data.content:
## .NET 8 Migration: BackgroundProcessor

### Key Breaking Changes

1. **`Thread.Abort()` throws `PlatformNotSupportedException`** in .NET Core+
2. **`Thread.ResetAbort()` is also removed** — no abort/reset pattern exists
3. **`ThreadAbortException` will never be raised** by the runtime

### Migrated Code

```csharp
public class BackgroundProcessor : IDisposable
{
    private readonly CancellationTokenSource _cts = new();
    private Task? _workerTask;

    public void Start()
    {
        _workerTask = Task.Run(() => ProcessLoop(_cts.Token));
    }

    private async Task ProcessLoop(CancellationToken token)
    {
        while (!token.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            try
            {
                // Pass the token so blocking calls become cancellable
                var item = await GetNextItemAsync(token);
                await ProcessItemAsync(item, token);
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException) when (token.IsCancellationRequested)
            {
                // Graceful shutdown requested — replaces ThreadAbortException
                RollbackCurrentTransaction();
                break; // exit the loop cleanly
            }
            finally
            {
                FlushBuffers();
            }
        }
    }

    public async Task StopAsync(TimeSpan? timeout = null)
    {
        _cts.Cancel();

        if (_workerTask is not null)
        {
            // Wait with a timeout instead of Thread.Sleep + Abort
            var completed = _workerTask.WaitAsync(
                timeout ?? TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

            try
            {
                await completed;
            }
            catch (TimeoutException)
            {
                // Log: worker didn't stop in time — no equivalent to Abort().
                // Consider Environment.FailFast() only for truly stuck threads.
            }
            catch (OperationCanceledException)
            {
                // Expected — worker honored cancellation
            }
        }
    }

    public void Dispose()
    {
        _cts.Cancel();
        _cts.Dispose();
    }
}
```

### Migration Pitfalls

| Pitfall | Detail |
|---|---|
| **No forced termination** | .NET 8 has no `Thread.Abort`. All exit paths must be *cooperative* via `CancellationToken`. If `GetNextItem()` blocks without token support, your app hangs on shutdown. |
| **Blocking calls** | Any synchronous blocking call (e.g., `queue.Take()`) must be replaced with a cancellable async variant (e.g., `channel.ReadAsync(token)`). |
| **`volatile bool` is insufficient** | A `volatile bool` can't interrupt blocking I/O. `CancellationToken` integrates with async APIs, `WaitHandle`, and polling. |
| **`catch (ThreadAbortException)`** | This catch block becomes dead code — it *silently never executes*. Replace with `catch (OperationCanceledException)`. |
| **`Thread.ResetAbort()`** | Compiles but throws `PlatformNotSupportedException` at runtime. Delete it. |
| **Fire-and-forget `Task.Run`** | Unlike background threads, unobserved `Task` exceptions are swallowed. Store the `Task` and `await` it during shutdown. |

### If You Can't Make Blocking Calls Async

For legacy sync code that can't be changed (e.g., third-party SDK):

```csharp
// Wrap the blocking call with a cancellation-aware timeout
var item = await Task.Run(() => GetNextItem(), token);
```

> ⚠️ This doesn't actually *cancel* the blocked thread — it just stops waiting. The thread leaks until the call returns naturally. This is the best you can do without `Thread.Abort`.

### Also Consider: `BackgroundService`

For hosted apps (ASP.NET Core, Worker Services), prefer the built-in pattern:

```csharp
public class MyProcessor : BackgroundService
{
    protected override async Task ExecuteAsync(CancellationToken stoppingToken)
    {
        while (!stoppingToken.IsCancellationRequested)
        {
            var item = await GetNextItemAsync(stoppingToken);
            await ProcessItemAsync(item, stoppingToken);
        }
    }
}
// Register: builder.Services.AddHostedService<MyProcessor>();
```

This gives you automatic lifetime management, graceful shutdown integration, and logging — all wired into the host's `IHostApplicationLifetime`.
